Single-database configuration for Flask.

The historical migration chain through revision b6e1d8f3a2c4 is forward-only.
Those revisions can adopt tables, columns, indexes, and constraints created by
older runtime schema synchronization, so they cannot determine object ownership
well enough to downgrade without risking pre-existing data. Restore a verified
pre-migration database backup instead of running `flask db downgrade` across
this boundary.

These adoption revisions require live schema and data inspection, so Alembic
offline/`--sql` generation is intentionally unsupported. Stop all application
instances, take and verify a backup, and run one online migrator at a time. Test
the same command against an isolated database copy before touching production.

For MySQL 8.0.21+, use the repository validator only against an isolated,
explicitly authorized instance exposed on `127.0.0.1`. Store the administrator
credential in a local `mysql_config_editor` login path; do not put the password
in a command, environment variable, or repository file.

```powershell
& 'C:\Program Files\MySQL\MySQL Server 8.0\bin\mysql_config_editor.exe' set `
  --login-path=codex-medroom --host=127.0.0.1 --port=3306 --user=root --password

& backend\.venv\Scripts\python.exe scripts\validate_mysql_alembic.py `
  --login-path=codex-medroom --probe

& backend\.venv\Scripts\python.exe scripts\validate_mysql_alembic.py `
  --login-path=codex-medroom --expected-server-uuid=<uuid-from-probe>
```

The second command creates only random, scenario-specific databases and a
random local account. Account attributes bind the exact database names, run ID,
and server UUID; cleanup refuses to delete resources if those attributes no
longer match. Binary logging is rejected by default. Add `--allow-binlog` only
after accepting that the temporary DDL and account metadata will be logged.

Before a production cutover, run the read-only provider/application-account
check as well:

    python scripts/check_production_database.py

Packaged releases expose the same guard and a protected migration command:

    medical_room --check-database
    medical_room --migrate-database --backup-file /path/to/backup.zip --yes

The application download is one ZIP containing the SQL and its manifest. The
command also accepts an externally generated SQL file with an adjacent
`<backup.sql>.manifest.json`. The packaged migration streams the SQL, recomputes
its size and SHA-256, verifies that the manifest is bound to the configured
MySQL host/port/database, and performs
the read-only production preflight again after upgrade. SQLite data
copy is separate and defaults to dry-run:

    medical_room --import-sqlite /path/to/app.db

Only rerun it with `--execute --yes --expected-source-sha256=<dry-run hash>`
after stopping every writer. Never use a stale SQLite file as a rollback target
after MySQL has accepted production writes.
